      <section id="levels" class="section">
        <h2>Aerosol Warehouse Storage Classification: Levels 1, 2, and 3</h2>
        <p>Establishing the correct aerosol warehouse storage classification is the first step in designing a fire protection system. NFPA 30B divides aerosol products into three distinct hazard levels based on the chemical heat of combustion of the formulation. Level 1 represents the lowest hazard, typically reserved for water-based products with minimal flammable content. Level 2 covers products with moderate flammability, while Level 3 covers the highest-hazard formulations, which are filled with high concentrations of liquefied petroleum gases (LPG) or flammable solvents. To understand the thermodynamics and flash point behaviors of solvent components, read our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/flash-point-explained" target="_top">flash point and flammability testing</a>.</p>
        <p>This classification is not based on the volume of the can, but on the energy released during combustion. Water-based personal care mousse products, which contain high percentages of water and minimal propellant, release less heat and fall into Level 1 or 2. Conversely, solvent-based paint sprays or industrial lubricants, containing high percentages of propane, butane, and hydrocarbons, release immense energy and fall into Level 3. These levels dictate the allowable storage heights, pallet arrangements, and proximity to building walls.</p>

