      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section id="sprinklers" class="section">
        <h2>Sprinkler Design Aerosol Warehouse Requirements</h2>
        <p>Designing the fire suppression system requires strict compliance with sprinkler design aerosol warehouse rules. Standard wet-pipe sprinkler systems designed for ordinary commodities are insufficient for Level 2 and Level 3 aerosols. If a fire starts in a Level 3 storage zone, the intense heat generation will open multiple sprinkler heads simultaneously, overloading the water supply. To prevent this, facilities must use early-suppression fast-response (ESFR) sprinkler heads and high-pressure water delivery systems. To cross-reference specific sprinkler testing standards and hazard categories, consult the official <a href="https://www.nfpa.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NFPA</a> portal.</p>
        <p>In addition to ceiling-level sprinklers, in-rack sprinklers are required for high-pile storage configurations. In-rack sprinklers deliver water directly to the center of the racking, suppressing flames before they can reach upper pallets. Water flow rates, discharge angles, and sprinkler spacing must be calculated using hydraulic design software. If the sprinkler density is too low, the fire will overwhelm the system, causing container rocketing and spreading fire throughout the warehouse.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary); background: #f0f9ff; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--primary); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Key Insight</strong>
          ESFR sprinklers are critical because they deliver large, high-pressure water droplets that can penetrate the hot updraft of a fast-growing aerosol fire. Standard sprinklers produce smaller droplets that evaporate before reaching the core of the fire.
        </div>
      </section>

