      <!-- Section 4 -->
      <section id="compliance" class="section">
        <h2>NFPA Aerosol Fire Safety Standard Compliance Steps</h2>
        <p>Achieving compliance with the NFPA aerosol fire safety standard requires a systematic review of the storage facility. The first step is to perform a detailed inventory audit, identifying the classification level of every aerosol SKU stored in the facility. This classification data must be documented in a central register, listing the manufacturer SDS, the chemical heat of combustion, and the total volume of inventory. For details on workplace safety standards and chemical handling requirements, see the official regulatory guidelines on <a href="https://www.osha.gov/flammable-liquids" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA Flammable Liquids Safety</a>.</p>
        <p>Once the inventory is classified, the storage layout must be updated to match the maximum allowable quantities (MAQ) defined by NFPA 30B. Storing Level 3 aerosols beyond these limits requires separating them into specialized fire areas. These areas must be enclosed by fire-resistance-rated walls, and feature containment curbs, drainage systems, and automatic fire doors. Implementing these physical barriers isolates the hazard, preventing a fire in the aerosol zone from spreading to the rest of the facility.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--amber); background: #fffbeb; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--amber); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Rule of Thumb</strong>
          Always segregate Level 3 aerosols from other flammable liquids and general commodities. Place Level 3 inventory in designated, mesh-fenced containment zones to catch rocketing cans if a rupture occurs.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <section id="chemistry" class="section">
        <h2>Physical Chemistry: Heat of Combustion and Flammability Limits</h2>
        <p>The physical chemistry behind aerosol classifications is based on the chemical heat of combustion and the flammability limits of the ingredients. The heat of combustion is the total energy released as heat when a substance undergoes complete combustion with oxygen. During an incident, this value determines the heat release rate (HRR) and the growth rate of the fire. The thermodynamic properties of common aerosol propellants and organic solvents can be cross-referenced in the peer-reviewed <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubChem</a> database.</p>
        <p>For Level 3 aerosols, the high concentration of hydrocarbon propellants creates flammability ranges (typically between 1.8% and 9.5% by volume in air). If a leak occurs in an enclosed storage room, a spark can trigger a deflagration. Therefore, facilities must install continuous gas detection systems and explosion-proof electrical fixtures. These sensors must link to ventilation systems, automatically increasing airflow to dilute gas concentrations if a leak is detected.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <section id="design" class="section">
        <h2>Aerosol Fire Protection Standard and Facility Design</h2>
        <p>Designing a compliant storage facility requires incorporating the aerosol fire protection standard into the initial architectural planning. Building codes require specific structural parameters for facilities storing large volumes of Level 2 and Level 3 aerosols. This includes installing explosion-relief venting on exterior walls, using non-combustible roofing materials, and setting up dedicated drainage channels to handle fire protection water runoff. To review transport classification and safety regulations for shipping finished products, see our guide on the <a href="https://unece.org/dangerous-goods" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UN Recommendations on Dangerous Goods</a>.</p>
        <p>Drainage design is critical because burning liquefied propellants can float on water, spreading flames across the warehouse floor. Compliant facilities feature sloped floors and containment trenches that redirect water to an outdoor retention basin. Additionally, emergency responders must have access to fire hydrants, control valves, and foam systems. By integrating these safety features, facilities can manage high-intensity fires, protecting neighbors and the environment.</p>
      </section>

