        <h2 style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 24px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; color: var(--text-primary);">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px;">
          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What is the primary purpose of the NFPA 30B standard for aerosol products?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">The primary purpose of the NFPA 30B standard is to establish fire protection and storage requirements for facilities that manufacture, store, or display aerosol products. Because pressurized aerosol cans represent a unique fire hazard during thermal exposure (due to potential container rocketing and rapid propellant release), the standard defines specific containment, sprinkler, and segregation rules to mitigate high-intensity fire risks.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">How does NFPA 30B classify aerosol products into hazard levels?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">NFPA 30B classifies aerosol products into three levels (Level 1, Level 2, and Level 3) based on their chemical heat of combustion. Level 1 aerosols contain low quantities of flammable propellants or solvents (heat of combustion ≤ 20 kJ/g), representing low hazards. Level 2 aerosols possess moderate hazards (20 to 30 kJ/g), while Level 3 aerosols represent the highest risk (> 30 kJ/g) due to high concentrations of flammable propellants like LPG.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What are the sprinkler design requirements for storing Level 3 aerosols?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Storing Level 3 aerosols requires high-density fire protection systems. This typically involves early-suppression fast-response (ESFR) ceiling sprinklers, often supplemented by in-rack sprinkler configurations. The system must deliver large water flow rates at high pressure to quickly absorb thermal energy, control potential container rocketing, and suppress fire propagation before structural containment is breached.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">How is the chemical heat of combustion calculated for aerosol flammability levels?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">The chemical heat of combustion is calculated by summing the mass fractions of each ingredient multiplied by their respective individual heats of combustion, as determined by laboratory oxygen bomb calorimeter testing. If a product's combined heat of combustion exceeds 30 kJ/g, it is automatically classified as a Level 3 flammable aerosol under NFPA 30B guidelines.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Does OSHA enforce NFPA 30B storage standards in manufacturing plants?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Yes, OSHA enforces workplace safety requirements for flammable aerosols under 29 CFR 1910.106. In many jurisdictions, local fire codes (such as the International Fire Code or NFPA 1 Fire Code) incorporate NFPA 30B by reference. Consequently, regulatory inspectors mandate compliance with NFPA 30B classification and warehouse storage rules to ensure general duty safety standards.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="padding-bottom: 8px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What is the rocketing effect of aerosol cans during a warehouse fire?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">The rocketing effect occurs when an aerosol can is exposed to intense heat, causing the internal pressure to exceed the container's burst limit. If the can ruptures, the escaping liquefied propellant vaporizes instantly, acting as a thruster that launches the container across the warehouse. NFPA 30B mandates fire protection measures (such as chain-link mesh barriers) to contain rocketing cans and prevent the spread of fire.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
